\label{permutation:lemma}
Given two matrices $A$ and $B$ of arbitrary sizes, there exist two permutation
matrices $P$ and $F$, which only depend on the dimensions of the
matrices $A$ and $B$, such that
$$
B\otimes A=P(A\otimes B)F\,.
$$
In the case in which $A$ and $B$ are square matrices of sizes
$n$ and $c$ respectively, the permutation matrices are related by \textnormal{$P=F^\dagger$}
where
$$
F=\left(\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c}
f &h f & h^2f &\cdots&h^{c-1}f\end{array}\right),
$$
and $h$ and $f$ are the following matrices of dimensions $cn\times cn$ and $cn\times n$ respectively:
$$
h=\begin{pmatrix}
                   0 &\hspace{-0.2cm}1  &   \\
                     & \hspace{-0.2cm}0  & \hspace{0.1cm} 
